a portrait and a typed, voiced line costs one call.\",\n  \"Yes, a few times. A shape material string with a pattern name that isn't a pattern ('rock'), the default text outline that smears dark text on light keycaps, label() returning the action name for mouse users when the action has no mouse button, and glyphs like a middle dot and a degree sign that the pixel font can't draw. Each one cost a screenshot to find.\",\n  \"The mission framework: chapters as data with beats run as generator scripts, each beat a checkpoint, plus the M toolkit (say, objective, act, waitAct, reach, cook, wave). The cook minigame and the attract bot came next.\",\n  \"Seeing the result. The engine is large, so knowing whether something looked right meant building and screenshooting in a browser. Also, nothing tells a level designer that a spot is unreachable (props placed in a one-cell aisle).\",\n  \"I would overhaul the default text rendering. Make outline off by default (or pick it from contrast) and support or warn about missing glyphs like the middle dot, en and em dashes, and the degree sign, instead of silently drawing a question mark.\",\n  \"A built-in reachability check: map.reachable(from, to) or a debug overlay showing which acts, spawns and objective markers the flow field can't reach. Two of the four critical bugs were exactly that.\"\n ],\n \"prompt\": [\n  \"It asks for a complete vertical slice with real infrastructure (attract mode, controller-first 10-foot UI, glyph switching, axis-inversion toggles, a touch-only gate, hub multiplayer) instead of just a game. That pushes toward something that could actually ship.\",\n  \"How multiplayer should work standalone versus in the hub ('for the game on its own') was open to interpretation, so I chose local drop-in co-op outside the hub and ghost mode inside it. Also, the title instruction to pick at random from 50 names gave a title (Breaking Bond) that fits a chemistry theme only by luck.\",\n  \"Fitting the whole scope into