{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgfDwsTVhZECsYpTMZphh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've built **Breaking Bond** (title chosen at random from 50 brainstormed names, #37) and bundled it into one file in this folder's root: `BreakingBond-BreakingBad-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261102-181322.html` (about 1.4 MB, works offline). The dev files and build script are in the subfolder of the same name, and `exit-interview.json` parses as JSON with every field filled in.\n\n**What's in it** (all tested in headless Chromium; no real controller or real iPhone was available):\n- **Story mode:** six chapters with an original cast:\n  1. a classroom quiz, ending in the diagnosis;\n  2. a stealth robbery of the school storeroom;\n  3. the cook minigame (measure, heat, stir, crystallize; 96% and up comes out blue);\n  4. a junkyard deal that turns into a fight, using thrown flasks (one explodes, one releases choking gas);\n  5. night deliveries in town under DEA patrols, with a heat meter and dumpsters to hide in;\n  6. a two-phase boss fight. A \"Catalyst\" hat mode makes enemies scatter.\n- **Between chapters:** an upgrade screen, checkpoints, autosaves, and Continue and Chapters on the title screen.\n- **Cook-Off:** a score-attack mode for 1–2 players on one machine. In an AI Hub session it runs as a 1–4 player \"ghost\" round: everyone plays their own copy and sees the other players' characters.\n- **Attract mode:** after 10 idle seconds on the title, a bot plays scenes from the game; any input exits.\n- **Controls and screens:** PlayStation glyphs by default, with an Xbox option in Settings, plus toggles to invert the sticks left/right and up/down. Prompts switch to keys and mouse buttons when you use them, and the mouse can click things in the world. On touch-only devices the title shows \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the Settings button, and the attract mode still runs.\n- **Camera:** three-quarter view by default; V or R3 switches to top-down or isometric.\n- **Credits:** the About & Credits screen holds the four sections below.\n\n**Testing:** a bot completes all six chapters, the AI Hub harness check passes in all three scenarios (2 players; 4 players with lag and packet loss; the host leaving), and 24 phone, tablet and desktop sizes in Chromium, WebKit and Firefox came back clean. Testing caught about 15 problems, all fixed.\n\n## Source Information\n- Date: 2026-11-02 18:13\n- Source: Breaking Bad (2008-2013), the AMC series created by Vince Gilligan\n- Genre and Perspective: crime action-adventure with a chemistry cook minigame, stealth, arena combat and a boss fight; the engine's native three-quarter top-down view, switchable in play to top-down and isometric\n- Tech: my-3D2dge v0.85.1 (full edition), plain JavaScript, AI Hub session client 1.3.0 (protocol v1), bundled with a small Node build script; no other libraries\n- Art Direction: the engine's native PS1-era pixel look at 320x240, expanded to fill the screen; HD humanoid rigs, textured tile walls and floors, data-defined 3D shapes (RV, cars, lab benches, crusher), night lighting, particles\n- Animation: the engine's procedural humanoid rigs, posed from motion; built-in attack moves with wind-up and follow-through, poses, roll afterimages, plus a code-drawn hat that follows the head\n- Audio: all music written as chip-synth note strings (9 songs, layered chase music in stealth), sound effects defined in code, Animalese-style gibberish voices made letter by letter, a different voice per character\n- Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 players (positions sent between copies, progress and final score reported); local drop-in 2-player co-op outside the hub\n- Testing: headless Chromium through Playwright 1.63 (scene smoke runs, bot playing all 6 chapters, scripted-input screenshots), viewport-matrix (24 viewports, 3 engines), AI Hub harness (2 players; 4 players with lag and loss; host leaving): all pass\n- Research: none beyond the engine handoff docs and the AI Hub README; the show's premise and tone are from general knowledge\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 15. Among them: a crash that blocked chapter 1, two chapters you couldn't finish (chapter 5's customers stood inside houses; props blocked chapter 2's storeroom aisles), guards catching you during dialog, the camera not following during dialog, unreadable keycap text, mouse players seeing \"INTERACT\" instead of E, characters the pixel font can't draw, the touch-only notice appearing late, an AI partner who won fights alone, checkpoint restarts leaving extra characters behind, and a hard-to-see Cook-Off HUD.\n- **How many were critical or game-breaking?** 4: the chapter 1 crash, the two unfinishable chapters, and being caught during dialog.\n- **Most helpful test:** the autoplay test. The attract-mode bot played every chapter start to finish while I logged each step, and it found the crash and both dead ends.\n- **Least helpful test:** the first screenshot pass, taken before the camera fix. Most frames just showed the same camera bug again.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most?** The standard features come built in: menus, settings, rebinding, glyph sets, saves, the HTML HUD, dialog with portraits, guard sight and suspicion, pathfinding, and good-looking characters out of the box.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes, several times:\n  - a material pattern name that doesn't exist;\n  - the default text outline, which smears dark text on light backgrounds;\n  - the prompt label falling back to the action's name for mouse users;\n  - characters the pixel font can't draw (middle dot, degree sign).\n- **The most important thing I built myself:** the mission framework. Chapters are data, each step is a script that doubles as a checkpoint, plus a small toolkit for dialog, objectives and waves. After that came the cook minigame and the attract bot.\n- **The hardest part:** checking results only by building and taking browser screenshots. Nothing warns a level designer that a spot can't be reached.\n- **What I'd overhaul:** text rendering. Outline should be off by default, and missing characters should get a warning instead of a silent \"?\".\n- **What I'd change:** add a built-in reachability check or overlay for objectives, spawn points and interactable objects. Two of the four critical bugs were unreachable spots.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What I liked most:** it asks for real game infrastructure (attract mode, a controller-first TV interface, glyph switching, stick inversion, a touch-only notice, hub multiplayer), not just a game.\n- **What was confusing:** what multiplayer should mean outside the hub (\"for the game on its own\"). I chose local co-op there and ghost mode in the hub. The random title pick also matched the chemistry theme only by luck.\n- **The hardest thing:** fitting six chapters, the cook minigame, stealth, combat, a boss, upgrades, Cook-Off, attract mode and the hub into one coherent slice on an unfamiliar engine, while keeping the screens readable from 10 feet away.\n- **The one test I'd have run:** a real DualSense and Xbox controller on a TV, going through every menu and a full chapter, to confirm glyphs, stick inversion and menu focus on real hardware instead of a simulated device."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1314,"cache_read_input_tokens":380996,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1314,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0af7f9d2-08b6-4da9-9a1b-3a3d42ba1fc2","uuid":"583af56a-3223-4208-bc14-2d0b37aba27e","timestamp":"2026-11-03T02:42:40.802Z","request_id":"req_011CgfDws1x6dEUfBLuMQZWG"}
