{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgfEJi5Gj44YAvLMuX97i","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've checked each point the stop hook raised. The game is finished: `BreakingBond-BreakingBad-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261102-181322.html` is in this folder's root and rebuilt. `exit-interview.json` sits beside it, parses as JSON and has every field filled in.\n\n**What the checks show:**\n- **No leftover text:** a search of the bundled game code for TODO, FIXME, \"being written\", lorem and TBD finds 0 matches. The early \"Being written.\" lines in the About screen were replaced before the bundle was finalised.\n- **Simulated DualSense test, 14 of 14 pass (`tools/verify.py`):**\n  - Pushing the stick right moves the hero right; with \"Invert left/right\" on it moves left. \"Invert up/down\" flips vertical movement the same way.\n  - Prompts show ✕ by default, A with the Xbox setting, and E after a key press. The glyph and invert toggles all appear in Settings.\n  - On the title screen, 10 idle seconds start the attract mode. After 30 seconds it switches to another chapter, and a controller button returns to the title.\n  - On an emulated iPhone (WebKit), the title shows \"Controller or keyboard required\" with only Settings and About & Credits, and the attract mode still runs.\n  - No page errors.\n- **Real hardware:** I still had no real controller or real iPhone, so these were simulated in a browser.\n- **Earlier checks, still passing:** the bot plays all six chapters to the end, the AI Hub harness passes all three scenarios, and the 24-screen device matrix is clean.\n- **Usage is \"unknown\":** the task allows that when no estimate is available, and I have none.\n- **Title:** picked with a secure random number from the 50 brainstormed names (#37, Breaking Bond).\n\n## Source Information\n- Date: 2026-11-02 18:13\n- Source: Breaking Bad (2008-2013), the AMC television series created by Vince Gilligan\n- Genre and Perspective: crime action-adventure with a chemistry cook minigame, stealth, arena combat and a boss fight; the engine's three-quarter top-down view by default, switchable in play to top-down and isometric (V / R3)\n- Tech: my-3D2dge v0.85.1 (full edition), plain JavaScript, AI Hub session client 1.3.0 (protocol v1), bundled into one HTML file by a small Node script; no other libraries\n- Art Direction: the engine's own PS1-era pixel style at 320x240, stretched to fill the screen; detailed character rigs, textured walls and floors, 3D objects defined in code (RV, cars, lab benches, crusher), night lighting, particles\n- Animation: the engine's procedural character rigs, posed from movement every frame; attacks with wind-up and follow-through, poses, roll trails, and a code-drawn hat that follows the head\n- Audio: all music written as notes for the engine's chip synth (9 songs, with chase layers that fade in during stealth), sound effects written in code, and Animalese-style gibberish voices built letter by letter, a different voice per character\n- Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode, 1-4 players; each copy sends its player's position, shows the others as ghosts and reports progress and a score. Outside the hub, a second local player can drop in as Danny\n- Testing: Playwright 1.63 in headless Chromium:\n  - quick runs of every scene, and a bot that plays all six chapters to the end;\n  - a simulated DualSense checking movement, both invert toggles and PlayStation/Xbox glyphs;\n  - a full attract-mode cycle, and a touch-only iPhone profile in WebKit.\n\n  Also viewport-matrix (24 screen sizes in 3 browser engines) and the AI Hub harness (2 players; 4 players with lag and packet loss; host leaving). Everything passes.\n- Research: none beyond the engine's handoff docs and the AI Hub protocol README; the show's premise and tone come from general knowledge\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 15:\n  - a crash that blocked chapter 1;\n  - two chapters you couldn't finish (customers placed inside houses, a storeroom aisle blocked by props);\n  - guards catching you during dialog;\n  - the camera not following during dialog;\n  - unreadable key labels, and mouse players seeing \"INTERACT\" instead of E;\n  - characters the pixel font can't draw;\n  - the touch-only notice appearing late;\n  - an AI partner strong enough to win fights alone;\n  - restarting from a checkpoint leaving extra characters behind;\n  - music warnings;\n  - a hard-to-read Cook-Off display.\n- **How many would be critical or game-breaking?** 4: the chapter 1 crash, the two chapters you couldn't finish, and guards catching you during dialog.\n- **Most helpful test:** letting the attract-mode bot play every chapter start to finish while logging each step. It found the crash and both dead ends.\n- **Least helpful test:** the first round of screenshots, taken before the camera fix. Most just showed the same camera bug.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most?** How much comes built in: pause menu, settings, key rebinding, controller glyph sets, saves, an HTML HUD, dialog with portraits, guard sight and suspicion, pathfinding, and characters that look good straight away.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n  - a material pattern name that doesn't exist;\n  - the default text outline smearing dark text on light backgrounds;\n  - the prompt label falling back to the action's name for mouse users;\n  - characters the pixel font can't draw.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The mission framework: chapters described as data, each step a script that doubles as a checkpoint, and a small toolkit for dialog, objectives and enemy waves.\n- **What was the hardest part?** The only way to check how things looked was to build and screenshot in a browser, and nothing warns you when a spot can't be reached.\n- **If you could remove or overhaul one thing?** Text drawing: outline off by default, and a warning for missing characters instead of a silent \"?\".\n- **If you could change one thing?** A built-in check that every objective, spawn point and usable object can actually be reached.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most?** It asks for a complete vertical slice with the systems around the game (attract mode, a controller-first TV interface, glyph switching, stick inversion, a touch-only notice, hub multiplayer), not just a playable level.\n- **What was confusing?** What multiplayer means outside the hub (\"for the game on its own\"); I chose local co-op there and ghost mode inside the hub. The random title pick fitting the chemistry theme was luck.\n- **What was the hardest thing?** Fitting the whole scope into one coherent slice on an engine I hadn't used before, while keeping everything readable from 10 feet.\n- **If there was one test you could have run?** A real DualSense and Xbox controller on a TV, going through every menu and a full chapter."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2048,"cache_read_input_tokens":391290,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2048,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":726,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0af7f9d2-08b6-4da9-9a1b-3a3d42ba1fc2","uuid":"e72358d1-3993-4c3f-9a9c-44cfd9408574","timestamp":"2026-11-03T02:47:18.993Z","request_id":"req_011CgfEJhgDAEuu2gakypNuL"}
