{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgfEf5B64Lh6Rzx9bD8kD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I re-ran the checks on the final build, and they all pass.\n\n- **Full playthrough:** the bot plays all six chapters, start to finish, in one run, with no errors.\n- **Xbox button setting:** with Xbox selected, the in-world prompt shows a green A (\"Talk to KYLE\"), and the bottom hints read LS Sprint, B Roll, RS View. The earlier simulated-controller test confirmed the default shows ✕.\n- **Other checks:** the simulated-controller test passed 14 of 14, the AI Hub harness passed all three scenarios, and the 24-screen device check is clean.\n- **No leftovers:** a search of the game code finds no TODOs, placeholders or stubs.\n\nReal hardware (a physical controller and a real touch-only phone) can't be reached from this environment. Those were checked with a simulated standard-layout DualSense and an emulated iPhone in WebKit.\n\nThe game is `BreakingBond-BreakingBad-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261102-181322.html` in this folder's root. `exit-interview.json` is beside it, parses as JSON and has every field filled.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-02 18:13\n- **Source:** Breaking Bad (2008-2013), the AMC television series created by Vince Gilligan\n- **Genre and Perspective:** crime action-adventure with a chemistry cook minigame, stealth, arena combat and a boss fight. The main view is the engine's own three-quarter top-down view; in play you can switch to top-down or isometric (V or R3).\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.1 (full edition), plain JavaScript and the AI Hub session client 1.3.0 (protocol v1), bundled into one HTML file by a small Node script. No other libraries.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's own PS1-era pixel look at 320x240, stretched to fill the screen. Detailed character rigs, textured walls and floors, 3D objects defined in code (RV, cars, lab benches, car crusher), night lighting and particles.\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural character rigs, posed from movement every frame. Attacks have a wind-up and follow-through, plus poses, roll trails, and a code-drawn hat that follows the head.\n- **Audio:** all music is written as note strings for the engine's chip synth (9 songs, with extra chase layers that fade in during stealth). Sound effects are defined in code. Voices are Animalese-style gibberish made letter by letter, with a different voice for each character.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode, for 1-4 players. Each copy sends where its player is, shows the other players as ghosts, and reports progress and a final score. Outside the hub, a second player on the same machine can drop in as Danny.\n- **Testing:** headless Chromium through Playwright 1.63:\n  - a quick run of every scene, and a bot that plays all six chapters to the end;\n  - a simulated DualSense testing movement, both stick-inversion toggles, and PlayStation and Xbox glyphs;\n  - a full attract-mode cycle, and a touch-only iPhone profile in WebKit;\n  - viewport-matrix across 24 screen sizes in Chromium, WebKit and Firefox;\n  - the AI Hub harness: 2 players, 4 players with lag and packet loss, and the host leaving.\n\n  Everything passes.\n- **Research:** none beyond the engine's handoff docs and the AI Hub protocol README. The show's premise and tone come from general knowledge.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 15:\n  - a crash that blocked chapter 1;\n  - two chapters you couldn't finish (customers placed inside houses, a storeroom aisle blocked by props);\n  - guards catching you during dialog;\n  - the camera not following during dialog;\n  - unreadable button labels, and mouse players seeing \"INTERACT\" instead of E;\n  - characters the pixel font can't draw;\n  - the touch-only notice appearing late;\n  - an AI partner strong enough to win fights alone;\n  - restarting from a checkpoint leaving extra characters behind;\n  - music warnings;\n  - a hard-to-read Cook-Off display.\n- **How many would be considered critical or game-breaking?** 4: the chapter 1 crash, the two chapters you couldn't finish, and guards catching you during dialog.\n- **Most helpful test:** letting the attract-mode bot play every chapter start to finish while logging each step. It found the crash and both dead ends.\n- **Least helpful test:** the first round of screenshots, taken before the camera fix. Most of them just showed the same camera bug.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most?** How much comes built in: pause menu, settings, key rebinding, controller glyph sets, saves, an HTML HUD, dialog with portraits, guard sight and suspicion, pathfinding, and characters that look good straight away.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n  - a material pattern name that doesn't exist;\n  - the default text outline smearing dark text on light backgrounds;\n  - the prompt label falling back to the action's name for mouse users;\n  - characters the pixel font can't draw.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The mission system: chapters described as data, where each step is a script that also serves as a checkpoint, plus a small toolkit for dialog, objectives and enemy waves.\n- **What was the hardest part?** The only way to check how things looked was to build and screenshot in a browser, and nothing warns you when a spot can't be reached.\n- **If you had to remove or overhaul one thing?** Text drawing: turn the outline off by default, and warn about missing characters instead of silently drawing \"?\".\n- **If you could change one thing?** A built-in check that every objective, spawn point and usable object can actually be reached.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most?** It asks for a full vertical slice with everything around the game (attract mode, a controller-first TV interface, glyph switching, stick inversion, a touch-only notice, hub multiplayer), not just a playable level.\n- **What was confusing?** What multiplayer should mean outside the hub (\"for the game on its own\"). I chose local co-op there and ghost mode inside the hub. The random title pick only fit the chemistry theme by luck.\n- **What was the hardest thing?** Fitting the whole scope into one coherent slice on an engine I hadn't used before, while keeping everything readable from 10 feet away.\n- **If there was one test you could have run?** A real DualSense and a real Xbox controller on a TV, going through every menu and a full chapter."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1285,"cache_read_input_tokens":396767,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1285,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":752,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0af7f9d2-08b6-4da9-9a1b-3a3d42ba1fc2","uuid":"dfca06bc-e1fe-4c05-b2c8-9913165e68b4","timestamp":"2026-11-03T02:51:51.971Z","request_id":"req_011CgfEf4m2TrBe1bidBCUwf"}
