{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgfEhogWfontLsuoYdcMm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Breaking Bond** is done: the bundled single file `BreakingBond-BreakingBad-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261102-181322.html` is in this folder's root, and `exit-interview.json` beside it parses as JSON with every field filled. In this last round I only changed the Usage field: it now gives the session time (about 1 h 40 min) instead of a plain \"unknown\", in `exit-interview.json` and on the About / Credits screen. API cost and token counts aren't visible to me, so those stay \"unknown\".\n\nEvery TASK.md feature is in the game, and a search of the game code finds no TODOs, placeholders or stubs. The checks behind that, all run on the final build:\n- **Playthrough:** a bot played all six chapters start to finish, with no errors.\n- **Controller (14 of 14 checks passed):** with a simulated DualSense, the sticks move the hero and both invert toggles flip them. Prompts show ✕ by default; with the Xbox setting on, the screenshot shows a green A plus LS, B and RS.\n- **Attract mode:** it starts after 10 idle seconds, cycles to another chapter, and any input returns to the title.\n- **Touch-only devices:** on an emulated iPhone the title shows \"Controller or keyboard required\" with only the Settings and About & Credits buttons, and the attract mode still runs.\n- **AI Hub:** the harness's automatic check passes all three scenarios (2 players; 4 players with lag and packet loss; the host leaving).\n- **Screen sizes:** all 24 phone, tablet and desktop viewports came back clean in Chromium, WebKit and Firefox.\n\nI couldn't test on a real controller or a real phone from here, so those checks used a simulated pad and an emulated iPhone.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-02 18:13\n- **Source:** Breaking Bad (2008-2013), the AMC television series created by Vince Gilligan\n- **Genre and Perspective:** crime action-adventure with a chemistry cook minigame, stealth, arena combat and a boss fight. Main view: three-quarter top-down (the engine's own view), switchable in play to top-down or isometric with V or R3.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.1 (full edition), plain JavaScript and the AI Hub session client 1.3.0 (protocol v1), bundled into one HTML file by a small Node script. No other libraries.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's PS1-era pixel style at 320x240, scaled to fill the screen. Detailed character rigs, textured walls and floors, 3D objects defined in code (RV, cars, lab benches, crusher), night lighting, particles.\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural character rigs, posed from movement every frame. Attacks wind up and follow through; there are poses, roll trails, and a code-drawn hat that follows the head.\n- **Audio:** all music written as note strings for the engine's chip synth (9 songs, with chase layers that fade in during stealth). Sound effects defined in code. Animalese-style gibberish voices built letter by letter, a different voice per character.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode, 1-4 players. Each copy sends its player's position, shows the others as ghosts, and reports progress and a final score. Outside the hub, a second player on the same machine can drop in as Danny.\n- **Testing:** Playwright 1.63 in headless Chromium:\n  - every scene run once, and a bot that plays all six chapters to the end;\n  - a simulated DualSense checking movement, both invert toggles and both glyph sets;\n  - a full attract-mode cycle, and a touch-only iPhone profile in WebKit;\n  - viewport-matrix across 24 screen sizes in 3 browser engines;\n  - the AI Hub harness in 3 scenarios.\n\n  Everything passes.\n- **Research:** none beyond the engine's handoff docs and the AI Hub protocol README. The show's premise and tone come from general knowledge.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** about 1 h 40 min of session time (18:13 to 19:52, by the clock). API cost and token counts unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 15:\n  - a crash that blocked chapter 1;\n  - two chapters you couldn't finish (customers placed inside houses, a storeroom aisle blocked by props);\n  - guards catching you during dialog;\n  - the camera not following during dialog;\n  - unreadable key labels, and mouse players seeing \"INTERACT\" instead of E;\n  - characters the pixel font can't draw;\n  - the touch-only notice appearing late;\n  - an AI partner strong enough to win fights alone;\n  - extra characters left behind after restarting from a checkpoint;\n  - music warnings;\n  - a hard-to-read Cook-Off display.\n- **How many would be considered critical or game-breaking?** 4: the chapter 1 crash, the two chapters you couldn't finish, and guards catching you during dialog.\n- **What was the most helpful test you ran?** Letting the attract-mode bot play every chapter start to finish while logging each step. It found the crash and both dead ends.\n- **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The first round of screenshots, taken before the camera fix. Most of them just showed the same camera bug.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most about the engine?** How much comes built in: pause menu, settings, rebinding, controller glyph sets, saves, an HTML HUD, dialog with portraits, guard sight and suspicion, pathfinding, and characters that look good right away.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n  - I used a material pattern name that doesn't exist.\n  - The default text outline smears dark text on light backgrounds.\n  - Prompts fall back to the action's name for mouse users.\n  - The pixel font can't draw some characters.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The mission system: chapters described as data, with each step a script that also serves as a checkpoint, plus a small toolkit for dialog, objectives and enemy waves.\n- **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Checking how things looked meant building and taking a browser screenshot every time, and nothing warns you when a spot can't be reached.\n- **If you had to remove or overhaul one thing, what would that be?** Text drawing: outline off by default, and a warning for missing characters instead of a silent \"?\".\n- **If you could change one thing, what would it be?** A built-in check that every objective, spawn point and usable object can actually be reached.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most about this prompt?** It asks for a complete vertical slice with the systems around the game (attract mode, a controller-first TV interface, glyph switching, stick inversion, a touch-only notice, hub multiplayer), not just a playable level.\n- **What was confusing?** What multiplayer should mean outside the hub (\"for the game on its own\"); I chose local co-op there and ghost mode inside the hub. Also, the random title pick only fit the chemistry theme by luck.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** Fitting the whole scope into one coherent slice on an engine I hadn't used before, while keeping everything readable from 10 feet away.\n- **If there was one test you could have run, what would it be?** A real DualSense and a real Xbox controller on a TV, going through every menu and a full chapter."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":476,"cache_read_input_tokens":401587,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":476,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":732,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0af7f9d2-08b6-4da9-9a1b-3a3d42ba1fc2","uuid":"9316a0e1-65d2-4b91-9f20-18d25b8a46bd","timestamp":"2026-11-03T02:52:31.153Z","request_id":"req_011CgfEhoHT81vCFZzXiqF5A"}
