ard-to-read Cook-Off display.\n- **How many would be critical or game-breaking?** 4: the chapter 1 crash, the two chapters you couldn't finish, and guards catching you during dialog.\n- **Most helpful test:** letting the attract-mode bot play every chapter from start to finish.\n- **Least helpful test:** the first round of screenshots, taken before the camera fix.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most about the engine?** How much comes built in: menus, settings, key rebinding, controller button glyphs, saves, an HTML HUD, dialog with portraits, guard sight and suspicion, pathfinding, and characters that look good straight away.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n  - I used a pattern name that doesn't exist;\n  - the default text outline smears dark text on light backgrounds;\n  - prompts fall back to the action's name for mouse players;\n  - the pixel font silently can't draw some characters.\n- **The most important thing I had to build myself:** the mission system. Chapters are described as data, and each step is a script that also serves as a checkpoint.\n- **The hardest part:** checking anything visual meant building and taking a browser screenshot, and nothing warns you when a spot can't be reached.\n- **One thing to remove or overhaul:** text drawing. The outline should be off by default, and missing characters should trigger a warning.\n- **One thing to change:** a built-in check that every objective, spawn point and usable object can actually be reached.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most about this prompt?** It asks for a complete vertical slice with the systems around the game, not just a level.\n- **What was confusing?** What multiplayer should mean outside the hub. Also, the random title pick fit the chemistry theme only by luck.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** Fitting the whole scope into one coherent slice on an engine I hadn't used before, while keeping everything readable from 10 feet.\n- **If there was 